# #RELEASE — Repository Release Contract

Use this prompt to plan, document, review, publish, correct, and hand off a
GitHub release for any product or feature in `devs-guide/openai`. It governs
repository releases, not the immutable dataset releases owned by `#DATA`.

## RELEASE-001 — Authority and activation

Activate `#RELEASE` only for an owner-authorized repository release or release
reconciliation. Record the repository, candidate version, previous published
tag, candidate commit, included products, intended audience, and publication
authority. A request to draft notes does not authorize a tag, merge, upload,
publication, or external announcement.

Apply repository and product contracts in their existing authority order.
Release work may describe accepted behavior but must not silently change that
behavior, expand permissions, or turn historical notes into live instructions.

## RELEASE-002 — Review the complete release range

Before writing, inspect the complete previous-tag-to-candidate commit range,
diff summary, operator-facing sources, schemas, configuration, publication
manifest, runtime dependencies, migrations, and validation evidence. Read at
least one prior well-structured release from the same repository for tone and
layout.

Build a release inventory that separates included behavior, supporting
changes, important exclusions, compatibility effects, safety effects, and
unresolved limitations. Every material release claim must be traceable to the
reviewed range or recorded validation; never infer completion from a branch
name, pull-request title, or draft note.

## RELEASE-003 — Version, tag, and correction integrity

Use the repository's approved semantic-version policy and bare numeric tags.
Treat every published tag and its target commit as immutable. Never move,
delete, recreate, or silently retarget a published tag, and never replace its
assets under the same version.

A reviewed title or body may be corrected in place only when the tag, target,
release state, and assets remain unchanged. A behavioral correction creates a
new version. Preserve declared migration baselines and distinguish the current
release, immediate predecessor, and older historical baselines.

## RELEASE-004 — Reader-focused language

Lead with the practical outcome and why it matters. Use plain, specific
language for the affected operator, developer, researcher, or reader. Describe
reusable behavior rather than centering a hostname, account, workstation,
private incident, or one deployment. Define uncommon terms and keep claims
proportional to the evidence.

Prefer a compact release that can be understood quickly. Do not pad notes with
raw diff statistics, exhaustive mechanical changes, private process history,
credentials, unverifiable superlatives, or generic celebration. Use tables,
diagrams, examples, and longer explanations only when they materially improve
operation, migration, or review.

## RELEASE-005 — Adaptive release body

Every body contains, in this order: `## X.Y.Z`; one or two summary paragraphs;
`### Scope`; `### Highlights`; `### Added`; `### Changed`; `### Fixed`;
`### #COMMIT`; `### Notable commits since <previous-tag>`; and `### Assets`.
Write `None` under an applicable change category with no entries rather than
removing its heading.

Add only the modules triggered by the release:

- `Who should use this release` and `Quick start` for a new user workflow.
- `Operator workflow and safety` for privileged, destructive, storage,
  authentication, network, purchase, or external-sharing behavior.
- `Interfaces and compatibility` for APIs, schemas, configuration, routes,
  file formats, or changed defaults.
- `Migration and recovery` when existing users must transition or can fail
  partway through an upgrade.
- `Validation evidence` when runtime, hardware, data, or human acceptance
  materially supports the claims.
- `Known boundaries` when exclusions or unsupported cases could otherwise be
  mistaken for supported behavior.

Keep the compact core when none of these modules is triggered. Never add a
module merely to make the release look larger.

## RELEASE-006 — Scope, history, and assets

Scope states the reviewed release range, product or feature boundary,
published entrypoints, and important exclusions. Highlights contain only the
highest-signal outcomes. Added, Changed, and Fixed explain behavior and impact,
not filenames without context.

The commit section records the approved release commit subject. Notable commits
use hashes verified from the actual release range and exact subjects; select
meaningful commits rather than dumping mechanical history. Assets distinguish
GitHub-generated source archives, Pages-published material, attached artifacts,
and artifacts intentionally absent. Never claim that source-only documentation
is an executable or binary asset.

## RELEASE-007 — Documentation and publication graph

Update every applicable source index, version record, feature record,
publication-manifest entry, rendered route, raw route, dependency declaration,
and validator in the same candidate. Keep source and rendered documentation
distinct, and publish byte-exact raw sources where the repository contract
requires them.

Do not publish generated build output on the source branch. A release is not
ready while a required entrypoint is absent from the publication graph, a live
route represents another commit, or documentation promises behavior that the
tagged source does not contain.

## RELEASE-008 — Pull request and candidate gate

Use a reviewable release branch and pull request. Freeze the exact candidate
commit, confirm the effective diff and dependency ancestry, and require the
repository's GitHub checks. For stacked releases, merge, deploy, tag, and
publish each predecessor before retargeting and advancing its dependent pull
request.

Use the approved merge strategy and release commit subject. Do not publish
from an unmerged branch, a moving reference, a dirty worktree, a failed check,
or a candidate whose reviewed notes describe another source state.

## RELEASE-009 — Draft, approval, and publication

Store the complete proposed body in a normal tracked Markdown file. Present
the exact title and body for human review before changing GitHub release state.
After approval, use that file without publication-time rewriting.

Deploy and validate the exact candidate before creating its tag. Create or
converge a draft first, verify its annotated tag, target commit, title, body,
draft and prerelease state, and asset list, then publish the same reviewed
draft. Read the public release back through the GitHub API. An idempotent retry
must fail closed when an existing release or tag points somewhere else.

## RELEASE-010 — Completion, handoff, and later changes

Complete only when the pull request is merged, required checks pass, Pages or
other publication surfaces match the tagged commit, the release is public,
and the live title, body, tag, target, state, and assets match the approved
record. Return links for the pull request, validation runs, deployment, tag,
release, and supported source archives plus any material limitation.

When the release enables a separate manual acceptance test, hand off the exact
tag, commit, immutable input, entrypoint, expected identities, bootstrap
instruction, and test boundary. Do not claim the downstream test passed until
its own evidence exists. Later behavioral fixes, refreshed artifacts, or
changed release inputs require a successor release.

